of White\'s Column F threat at the bottom. More importantly, F11 utilizes the existing Black stone at D9 to form an Open 3 (D9-E10-F11). This creates an immediate counter-threat that White must consider. While a perfect opponent will ignore this and play G10 to win on Row 10 immediately, creating a counter-threat maximizes the chance of an opponent\'s error compared to a purely passive block like G10 or F7.\nAnswer: {"col": "F", "row": 11}'}
